# We Welcome It

Many people genuinely did not know why Congressmen conducted the freedom struggle. Who could have expected that the freedom struggle was waged only to shackle the people of the country once it became free? The people believed that all their rights would be protected after the country attained freedom. But what is happening today? Shackles on the people's hands! A ban on the people's opinions! An obstacle to the growth of language! This is how affairs are being conducted today.

The government that banned the book *Ravana Kaviyam*, the government that has made imposing bans its very duty, has now banned the dramatic work *Iraniyan Allathu Inaiyatra Veeran* (*Iraniyan, or the Matchless Hero*) by poet Bharathidasan. When we condemn this, some people, with neither restraint in their tongues nor strength in their convictions, say that condemning the government at every turn has become our occupation. But the condemnation presented here is not ours. It is the condemnation offered by the Congress journal *Prasanda Vikatan*. Read it. For the attention of Congress supporters, we reproduce below what appeared under the title "We Welcome It" in the issue of *Prasanda Vikatan* dated 1 September 1948.

Within a few days of banning *Ravana Kaviyam*, the Madras government has banned another distinguished play. This play, written by the revolutionary poet Bharathidasan, has reportedly been performed on stage for many years. Its title is *Iraniyan Allathu Inaiyatra Veeran*.

When a government bans a book or a play, order and propriety require it to issue a statement explaining the reasons so that the general public may know them. We greatly regret that the Madras government has not done so.

Like the Madras government, the Bombay government has also banned books by scholars and sculptors of thought. The Bombay government has prohibited books imported from abroad for sale at a Bombay bookshop. The banned books include works by the world-renowned Maxim Gorky and Marx. In obstructing knowledge, the Madras and Bombay governments behave like twins born of the same mother.

It is the duty of the progressive forces in the country to oppose any government that obstructs the growth of the people's knowledge. The actions of both the Madras and Bombay governments are to be welcomed. Why do we welcome them? Because we believe that bans of this kind will hasten a revolution in thought within the country and pave the way for a complete transformation in the people's outlook. That is why we welcome this.

(*Dravida Nadu*, 5 September 1948)

---NOTES---
The final paragraph uses deliberate irony: the bans are "welcomed" because the writer expects repression to accelerate intellectual change. One OCR-damaged word before "actions" is omitted from the English because the sentence's governing meaning is otherwise clear.
